course: world
lesson: 173
title: "The game is rigged because I lost"
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Max loses a game of FIFA online and throws the controller. "This game is RIGGED! The other team was scripted to win!" His brother, watching: "Rigged? You let in four goals from corners. The script is called 'defend'." Losing is not proof of cheating.
explain: Grown-ups call it a self-serving bias: when we win, it's our skill; when we lose, it's the game, the controller, the lag, the referee, the banker. Lily wins one game and she's "a pro"; she loses five and "the game's broken".
explain: The fix is to test the excuse. Swap controllers: if your brother wins with yours, the controller works. Watch the replay: Finn's "speed boost from nowhere" was Finn crashing into a wall. Count: two penalties in fifty matches is not "always".
explain: Sometimes the excuse doesn't even fit the facts. In chess both players see the whole board, so the computer isn't "seeing your moves" by cheating. Kai blames lag online, but he loses offline too, on the same sofa.
explain: The mockery hits the excuse, not the loser: "Your controller works. Your driving's broken." Everyone loses. Saying "fair play, you were better" is the strong move.
fact: Losing does not prove cheating; the other player may be better, or luckier this time.
fact: Taking the credit for wins and blaming the game for losses is called a self-serving bias.
fact: In a fair game better players should win more often; that is the point of the game, not cheating.
fact: Test an excuse before believing it: swap controllers, watch the replay, count the times.
fact: Two times in fifty is a small number; count before you say "always".
fact: Chess has no hidden information: both players can see the whole board; a chess computer wins by calculating many moves ahead.
fact: Lag, a delay in an online game, can be real, but if you also lose offline, something else is going on, usually practice.
fact: In card games like Uno luck matters a lot, and sometimes one player gets amazing cards.
fact: In Monopoly the dice decide where you land; the banker handles the money but cannot choose squares.
fact: Replays show what really happened; look before you blame anyone.
fact: Winning shows some skill and some luck, and so does losing; be fair to yourself both ways.
fact: Words people use: "rigged" means secretly fixed so one side wins, "scripted" means planned in advance, "lag" is a delay in an online game, "fair play" means well done, you were better (UK), "sus" means suspicious, and "girl" or "bro" are just friendly words for a friend.
exercise: Max loses at FIFA online and says the game is rigged. What really happened? | He let in four goals from corners | The other team was scripted to win | The game was hacked by his brother | The referee was a bot that hates him
exercise: Ava loses Uno to her little cousin and says, "You cheated!" What is true? | In card games luck matters a lot, and sometimes one person gets great cards | Nobody can ever get many draw-fours | Six-year-olds always cheat at Uno | The cards must have been marked
exercise: Ben says the game "always" gives penalties against him. How many did he get in 50 matches? | Two | Fifty | Twenty-five | Ten
exercise: Chloe says her controller is broken. How did her brother test it? | He played with the same controller and won | He took the controller apart | He bought a new controller | He asked the shop
exercise: Oscar says the chess computer cheats because it can see all his moves. What is true? | Both players can see the whole board in chess | The computer reads his mind | The computer has a secret camera | Only the computer can see the whole board, because it is inside the game
exercise: How does a chess computer usually win? | By calculating many moves ahead | By secretly hiding some of your pieces | By moving twice when it's its turn | By seeing hidden information you don't have
exercise: Kai blames lag for losing online. What shows the excuse doesn't fit? | He loses offline too | He has a fast internet connection | His friends also have lag | The servers are close to him
exercise: Lily wins one game and says she's a pro; she loses five and says the game's broken. What is this called? | A self-serving bias | A fair judgement | The gambler's fallacy | Collective punishment
exercise: Jamal's mate says the game is rigged because the top players always win. What is true? | In a fair game, better players should win more often | Top players always pay to win | The game is set up to make the same few people win every single time | Top players never practise
exercise: Harper says her brother gets good Monopoly properties because he's the banker. What decides where you land? | The dice | The banker | The player with the most money | The oldest player
exercise: Finn says the other car got a speed boost from nowhere. What did the replay show? | He crashed into the wall | The other car had a secret boost | The game froze for a second | His controller was unplugged
exercise: What is the best way to check "the game cheated"? | Watch the replay or test it fairly | Shout until someone agrees | Throw the controller | Delete the game straight away and never play it again
exercise: What do grown-ups call "my wins are skill, my losses are the game"? | A self-serving bias | The sunk cost fallacy | Survivorship bias | A false choice
exercise: Before saying "always", what should you do? | Count the times | Say it louder | Ask the loser | Wait until you lose again
exercise: Could a banker in Monopoly cheat at all? | With the money, so checking the bank is fair, but not with the squares | Yes, by choosing where everyone lands | No, a banker can never cheat at anything | Only by changing the dice
test: His brother: "Rigged? You let in four goals from corners, bro. The script is called 'defend'." What is "scripted" supposed to mean? | Planned in advance so one side wins | Written in joined-up handwriting | Played very well | Shown on TV live
test: Cousin, age 6: "I didn't cheat, you shuffled." Everyone: "OOOOH." Why is that a killer comeback? | Ava shuffled the cards herself, so the deal was fair | The cousin admits cheating | Shuffling always helps the youngest player get the best cards | Ava never shuffles properly
test: Ben's mate: "Two out of fifty, that's always now, is it? Your maths is more rigged than the game." What is he mocking? | Calling two out of fifty "always" | Ben's football skills | Ben being bad at his maths lessons, as well as at games | Penalties in real football
test: Brother: "Your controller works. Your driving's broken." What would a fair player say next? | "Fair play, I need more practice." | "Your controller must be rigged too." | "I'm never playing this again." | "Let's say it was a draw."
test: Mum: "Oscar, you can see all ITS moves too. That's how chess works, darling." What would really help Oscar win? | Practice and learning simple tactics | A different board colour | Playing faster | Covering up the computer's pieces so it can't see them coming
test: His sister: "You lost to me on the same sofa, Kai. Is the sofa lagging?" What does "lag" mean? | A delay in an online game | A broken sofa | A type of controller | A cheat code
test: Friend: "You're a pro when you win and the game's broken when you lose? Same game, girl." Is calling her "girl" rude? | No, it's just a friendly word here | Yes, it's meant to really hurt her feelings | Yes, because it insults all girls everywhere | No, because Girl is actually her name
test: Jamal: "So it's rigged because the best people win? Bro just described sport." What is Jamal's point? | Better players winning is how fair games work | Sport is always rigged | Top players use cheats | Nobody can ever be the best at a game, so somebody must be cheating
test: Brother: "The dice decided, Harper. Unless you think I'm controlling dice with my brain." What would be a fair check? | Watch the banker's money, since squares are luck | Let Harper be the banker and roll for everyone | Remove the dice from the game | Give Harper all the best streets so that it's fair this time round
test: What would happen if Finn watched the replay before shouting? | He'd see his crash and not blame the game | He'd find a secret speed boost | The replay would show the game froze | He'd see the other car cheat by taking a secret shortcut through the wall
test: A clever one: in 100 online matches against players of his level, Max wins about 50. What does that suggest? | The game is fair; he wins about as often as expected | The game is rigged against him | The game is rigged for him | He should win every single match, so the game must be rigged against him
exam: Identify the principal error in concluding that a game is rigged on the ground that one has lost it. | A loss is treated as proof of cheating without other evidence | The game was insufficiently difficult | The opponent was too young | The match was played online
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning information available to players in chess. | Both players can observe the entire board at all times | Only the computer can observe the entire board | The computer's pieces are concealed from the human player until they move | The human player's moves are hidden
exam: Determine the term properly applied to attributing one's successes to skill and one's failures to external causes. | A self-serving bias | The gambler's fallacy | The sunk cost fallacy | Circular reasoning
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises two penalties awarded against a player in fifty matches? | A small number which does not justify the word "always" | Conclusive evidence of a rigged game | A majority of matches played | Evidence of a scripted referee
